 "reports_individuals_with_tnfaip3_variant": true,
 "relevance_note": "Mainly a mouse study, but it reports plasma sphingolipid results for one identifiable person: 'a single Tangier patient with compound heterozygous mutations in ABCA1'.",
 "method_note": "Built by hand with a short Python script (no table transcription; the paper has no tables). Only the single compound heterozygous Tangier patient gets a row, labelled 'Com Het' from the figure legends ('a compound heterozygous subject (Com Het, n = 1)'). The heterozygous ABCA1 carriers are described only as a group (means in Figures 1-2, no individual-level detail) and get no rows; whether they or the 'eight unaffected members of a Dutch family' are relatives of the Tangier patient is not stated. Contradiction: Methods say 'Six subjects with heterozygous mutations in ABCA1, one compound heterozygote ABCA1 and eight unaffected members of a Dutch family', but Figure 1 and 2 legends say 'Het, n = 8' and 'WT, n = 6'; n_patients_reported and cohort_size_as_stated are therefore left null (no total stated either). No ABCA1 variant descriptions are printed; two variant rows with variant_original_string 'not reported' are kept because the paper states compound heterozygosity (two variants) for the patient. The general phrase 'homo and heterozygous loss of function mutations' is not person-specific and was not used for pathogenicity or zygosity. Background statements about Tangier disease (HDL deficiency, elevated triglycerides, polyneuropathy) cite the literature and are not about this patient, so all checklist items are 'not reported'. Human subjects data were previously reported in Karuna et al., 2010/2011.",
